    "cursorSync": "Off",
    "description": "Manhattan's 22,324 buildings raised to their surveyed roof height and coloured by era, against the MTA subway's lines and stations: how much of the city stands within walking distance of a train. Data from a public GeoLens catalogue.",
    "editable": true,
    "elements": {
      "panel-1": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "id": 1,
          "links": [],
          "title": "",
          "transparent": true,
          "vizConfig": {
            "group": "text",
            "kind": "VizConfig",
            "spec": {
              "fieldConfig": {
                "defaults": {},
                "overrides": []
              },
              "options": {
                "content": "# Manhattan, by height and by subway\n\nTwo open datasets and one question: how much of Manhattan is actually within\nwalking distance of a train?\n\nThe buildings are NYC Open Data's footprints — 22,324 across Lower and Midtown,\neach with a surveyed roof height and a year of construction. The subway is the\nMTA's own open data: 29 services and 496 stations, with step-free access marked\non each. Neither was downloaded from its publisher: both came through a public\n[GeoLens](https://datasets.geolibre.app) catalogue, found by following a shared\nGeoLibre project, and are read here from its Parquet exports, through the\ngateway's cache.\n\nThree things worth knowing before you read the map:\n\n- **Press play on the map's clock.** Every building carries its year, so the\n  timeline runs from 1719 to today and the city rises as it plays.\n- **The years belong to the lot, not to the tower.** The file's second-tallest\n  building, 1,428 ft, is dated 1924 — a year when the tallest thing in New York\n  was the 792 ft Woolworth Building. `construction_year` records the earliest\n  structure on the site, not the one measured here.\n- **The areas here are recomputed.** The file ships a `shape_area` column\n  measured in Web Mercator, which at this latitude inflates every area by about\n  74 %. Every square metre on this dashboard is measured from the geometry in\n  UTM 18N instead.\n\nThe radius, the floor on height and the eras are dashboard variables: the\ncoverage figure, the table and the map all move together.",
                "mode": "markdown"
              }
            },
            "version": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "panel-2": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [
                {
                  "kind": "PanelQuery",
                  "spec": {
                    "hidden": false,
                    "query": {
                      "datasource": {
                        "name": "duckdb"
                      },
                      "group": "motherduck-duckdb-datasource",
                      "kind": "DataQuery",
                      "spec": {
                        "format": 1,
                        "rawSql": "SELECT\n  bin,\n  height_roof AS height_ft,\n  height_roof * 0.3048 AS height_m,\n  era,\n  construction_year,\n  -- The map's clock. Years before 1600 are the file's way of saying \"no idea\",\n  -- and a NULL keeps those buildings out of the animation rather than parking\n  -- them all on one impossible date.\n  CASE WHEN construction_year \u003e= 1600\n       THEN make_timestamp(construction_year, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0) END AS time,\n  round(ST_Area(ST_Transform(geometry, 'EPSG:4326', 'EPSG:32618', always_xy := true))) AS footprint_m2,\n  ST_AsText(geometry) AS geom\nFROM read_parquet('http://feeds/geolens/datasets/4a0bd0db-cd92-424a-8a2e-62d270ae918a.parquet')\nWHERE height_roof \u003e= CAST($minHeight AS DOUBLE)\n    AND era IN (${era:singlequote})"
                      },
                      "version": "v0"
                    },
                    "refId": "A"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "kind": "PanelQuery",
                  "spec": {
                    "hidden": false,
                    "query": {
                      "datasource": {
                        "name": "duckdb"
                      },
                      "group": "motherduck-duckdb-datasource",
                      "kind": "DataQuery",
                      "spec": {
                        "format": 1,
                        "rawSql": "SELECT service, service_name, ST_AsText(geometry) AS geom\nFROM read_parquet('http://feeds/geolens/datasets/fd3a8635-1f38-4b51-a18f-784ea84abbbf.parquet')"
                      },
                      "version": "v0"
                    },
                    "refId": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "kind": "PanelQuery",
                  "spec": {
                    "hidden": false,
                    "query": {
                      "datasource": {
                        "name": "duckdb"
                      },
                      "group": "motherduck-duckdb-datasource",
                      "kind": "DataQuery",
                      "spec": {
                        "format": 1,
                        "rawSql": "SELECT\n  stop_name,\n  daytime_routes AS routes,\n  CASE ada WHEN 1 THEN 'Full' WHEN 2 THEN 'Partial' ELSE 'None' END AS step_free,\n  structure,\n  ST_Y(ST_Centroid(geometry)) AS latitude,\n  ST_X(ST_Centroid(geometry)) AS longitude\nFROM read_parquet('http://feeds/geolens/datasets/c646ff35-82eb-442f-a524-efcbb1645023.parquet')"
                      },
                      "version": "v0"
                    },
                    "refId": "C"
                  }
                }
              ],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "description": "Colour is the era it went up; height is the surveyed roof, in metres. Press play to watch the city arrive.",
          "id": 2,
